<node id="Table 1: Maximum path lengths, per-layer complexity and minimum number of sequential operations for different layer types (table)">
  <data key="d0">Table 1: Maximum path lengths, per-layer complexity and minimum number of sequential operations for different layer types (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">The table compares the computational complexity and path length between different types of layers: Self-Attention, Recurrent, Convolutional, and Self-Attention (restricted). It shows that Self-Attention layers are faster than Recurrent layers when the sequence length is smaller than the representation dimensionality, which is most often the case with sentence representations used by state-of-the-art models in machine translations. It also shows that convolutional layers are generally more expensive than recurrent layers, but separable convolutions decrease the complexity considerably, and it highlights the benefits of using self-attention layers over recurrent and convolutional layers in terms of computational efficiency and path length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-089cfbd4d2f2a85c3880f2d9dd19feba</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763833656</data>
</node>
<node id="Positional Encoding Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Positional Encoding Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation represents the positional encoding for a given position pos in a sequence of length d_model, where d_model is the dimensionality of the model. The positional encoding is a sinusoidal function that encodes the relative position of each token in the sequence. The sine and cosine functions are used to create a smooth and continuous encoding that can be used to represent the order of the sequence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d0c4cd5f282f346fb04e048e557a24a</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763833656</data>
</node>
<node id="Learning Rate Schedule (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Learning Rate Schedule (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents a learning rate schedule for training models. It is a function of the model's dimensionality, the current step number, and a warmup step count. The equation is designed to gradually increase the learning rate over time, starting from a low value and then increasing it more rapidly as the model progresses through its training.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-964b9709626716bfefc7fa402f34f132</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763833656</data>
</node>
<node id="Table 2: BLEU scores and training costs for various models (table)">
  <data key="d0">Table 2: BLEU scores and training costs for various models (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table compares the BLEU scores and training costs of various models for English-to-German and English-to-French translation tasks. The 'Transformer (big)' model achieves the highest BLEU scores and the lowest training costs for both tasks, while the 'ByteNet [18]' model has the lowest BLEU scores and the highest training costs for both tasks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cc05458d2e1470e58d823fa7f0f084cc</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763833656</data>
</node>
<node id="computational resources for training deep learning models (discarded)">
  <data key="d0">computational resources for training deep learning models (discarded)</data>
  <data key="d1">discarded</data>
  <data key="d2">The discarded content provides specific values for the theoretical maximum floating-point operations per second (TFLOPS) for various NVIDIA GPUs, which are K80, K40, M40, and P100. These values are used to estimate the number of floating point operations used to train a model, which is a critical factor in understanding the computational cost of training deep learning models. The context of this content is the broader discussion of training deep learning models, which is a significant topic in the field of artificial intelligence and machine learning. The content is useful for knowledge retrieval as it provides specific information about the computational resources required for training deep learning models, which can help researchers and practitioners understand the trade-offs between computational resources and model performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d78d14bf757ad8ccfabbfedf57c9d5a2</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763833656</data>
</node>
